// CORPORATE KOMBAT — RUMBA ROOM — one song: what it plays, how it is arranged, how it sounds.
//
// CORPORATE KOMBAT as a flamenco rumba, Gipsy Kings style — a game alternate of office, selectable
// from the dev menu (GAME ALTERNATES) and on the desk. Nothing replaces the live song.
// 
// Peter (25 Oct): "a bunch of remixes of cabinet songs that take advantage of the new guitar
// presets… something different." RUMBA ROOM: the corporate party that got out of hand.
// 
// The lobby has hired a classical guitarist: one nylon, G major, the lobby tune played politely
// (bars 1–4, looped on the cabinet screen). Then his band walks in — two more nylons strumming
// the rumba, a cajón, palmas, congas, a bass — and the lobby tune turns MINOR, which is exactly the
// old office hook (CORPORATE KOMBAT v2: G B♭ D B♭ C E♭ D B♭): the opening falseta of every loop.
// 
// THE HOOK is CORP FUNKHY 4's MELODY A (office.js lead25), note for note at its verse pitch, on a
// song-local nylon lead (harder nail near the bridge, LEGATO, 30 ms glide): where the Moog's notes
// overlap the guitar slurs, the rest are picked. A second nylon takes it in thirds in the choruses;
// in the final chorus a third doubles it an octave down.
// 
// HARMONY, the office's own, in open-position guitar shapes (one note per string):
//   A     | Gm · Cm | Gm7 | B♭(6) | Cm · D7 |
//   B     | E♭ | D | Cm7 | D7 |
//   SOLO  | Gm | F | E♭ | D |  ×2  — the Andalusian cadence
//   LOBBY | G · C · G | G · Am7 · D6 | C · Am7 · D6 | G · D7 |
//   END   | E♭ | Cm7 | Am7♭5 | D7 | then the hook's last phrase in stop-time onto G major
// 
// THE RUMBA HAND (two nylons, left and right, eighths):
//   BASIC  D (air) slap U  D (air) slap U   — down, down-up, the slap on 2 and 4
//   FULL   D  u    X    U  D  U    X    U   — the pendulum, the slaps choked (60 ms release)
// The slap is its own lane, the GOLPE (frppFunkMuted) on 2 and 4, so on the nylon lane the air
// up-stroke and the slap drop out as a pair and the Alternate strum stays in step. Up-strokes
// take the top three strings. The lanes are "Alternate · Up first" (the desk names a strum by
// pitch: UP is low to high, which is a guitarist's DOWN-stroke) with the returns 4 dB under.
// A third nylon, RASGUEADO, plays the four-finger roll (e-a-m-i, all down, 5 ms apart) into the
// downbeats that open phrases.
// 
//   1–4    LOBBY — G major, once (the cabinet screen loops it): one nylon guitar, fingerstyle, the lobby tune over a thumb bass, a D7 arpeggio home
//   5–8    ENTRADA — the band walks in: two nylons strumming the rumba, golpe, cajón, palmas, bass; the lead's falseta is the lobby tune in the MINOR — the old v2 hook — with a picado run down into
//   9–16   VERSO — MELODY A on the nylon lead, twice (the second ending climbs); one rhythm guitar, then two; palmas sordas join
//   17–24  ESTRIBILLO — MELODY A with the second nylon in thirds; FULL strum on the right, tresillo bass, congas, contratiempo palmas, rasgueado rolls
//   25–28  PUENTE — the B melody alone on the lead; E♭ → D, already Phrygian; a roll into
//   29–36  SOLO — picado solo over THE ANDALUSIAN CADENCE Gm–F–E♭–D, twice: phrased runs, then sixteenths to C6 and down through D Phrygian dominant; the bass descends; shaker
//   37–40  PALMAS — breakdown: palmas claras and sordas, the golpe, one strum a bar, the bass held, the lead on the hook's skeleton; the cajón and a rasgueado build back into
//   41–44  PUENTE — the B melody in thirds, the full band, into
//   45–52  ESTRIBILLO FINAL — the singalong: MELODY A in thirds AND an octave down, both rhythm guitars FULL, a roll every two bars, everything in; "¡olé!" on the way in; the last ending falls to A and the loop goes back to bar 5
//   53–56  LAST DANCE (after the loop) — the office's wind-down line on the lead: E♭ Cm7 Am7♭5 D7
//   57–60  REMATE — stop-time: the band hits on the one, the hook's last phrase alone between, a roll onto G MAJOR and "¡olé!"
// 
// Sounds: FRPP-6 throughout the strings — the nylon lead and segunda (song-local Flamenco Lead),
// the rhythm nylons (song-local Rumba Hand, plain and Shimmer), the lobby guitar (frppNylon), the
// golpe (frppFunkMuted), the bass (frppBassNeck). A song-local cajón (bass tone and slap, ghost taps
// on their own lane); palmas claras (clapHands) and sordas (song-local, cupped); congas high, low,
// slap; a shaker in the solo and the final chorus. "¡Olé!" is a JMJR-4 announcer saying "olay".
// No kit, no cymbals, no riser, no CRLS-1, no MRDR-3.
// 
// Levels measured (solo LUFS through the master chain, _remix-bounce --lanes) against the remix
// targets (kick ≈ lead, bass 1–3 under, snare 3–6 under, hats 12–16 under), final chorus 45–52:
//   lead −26.7, cajón bass −26.1, bass + sine −27.5, cajón slap −32.2, palmas claras −33.9, rumba
//   guitars −32.1 / −33.1, segunda −32.4, octave −35.2, shaker −39; verse 9–16 the lead −26.9 against
//   the cajón −26.1; the picado solo −24.4 (featured); the lobby guitar alone −27.0; the "¡olé!" about
//   5 dB under the lead while it sounds. Every fader within the desk's +6 dB.
// Bands against the cabinet median: sub −6 (a sine under the bass, a 60 Hz shelf), low-mid +4 and mid
// +5 (it is a record of nylon guitars; a 380 Hz dip on each), air −8 (nylon has little; shelves and an
// exciter on the strums and the lead, the shaker and palmas carry the top).
// Trailing master gain -0.3 dB for −21.0 LUFS integrated, the whole song (peak −2.8 dBFS).
// 60 bars at 115 (the office's tempo), straight, 125 s; the loop 5–52 is 100 s.
// Written by work/local/_office-remix-rumba-room.mjs through work/local/_remix-lib.mjs, which
// self-checks chord tones on the beats, passing notes stepping on, semitone clashes, one note per
// string, the Alternate strum parity, the loop wrap and the glide times; re-running it rewrites
// this file.
//
// The music below is the composition. Everything under THE DESK WRITES BELOW HERE
// is written by `npm run mixer` and will be rewritten on every save — put notes
// about the song up here, where they survive.
